{
  "requirement": "Inspection preserves the requested path scope and the selected language for\noutlining and structural search.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The subject `requirements.sdk.sources.provider_results` has two obligations: inspection preserves the requested path scope, and it preserves the selected language, for both outlining and structural search. I found a test with a real assertion for each. The `.parse_snapshot` and `.search_completeness` sub-requirements are separate requirements, and I did not assess them here.\n\n**Path scope**\n- `defaults_use_provider_primitives_and_keep_one_parse_snapshot` runs `search_text` with `SourceScope::under(\"src\")`. The `Memory` provider records `list:src`, and the test asserts that exact event sequence. Only matches under `src` come back, and the match path is `src/one.txt`.\n- `structural_search_uses_overridden_parses_their_snapshot_and_requested_scope` runs `search_structure` three times with `SourceScope::under(\"src\")`. The `CachedParse` provider records each scope passed to `list`, and the test asserts `[\"src\",\"src\",\"src\"]`. The provider lists both `src/one.rs` and `other/two.rs`, yet there are exactly 3 parses and the match path is `src/one.rs`. A search that ignored or widened the scope would fail those assertions.\n- `gitignore_is_shared_by_all_operations_with_or_without_a_git_repository` checks real-filesystem scope handling for text search. The scopes are `under(prefix)` for excluded paths, `under(\"..\")` (an error), and `under(\"./src/../src\")`, which normalises and excludes the `src-old` sibling. It runs with and without a git repository.\n- `scoped_inspection_skips_unrelated_directories_and_reports_relevant_ignore_errors` checks that a listing scoped to `src` returns only `src/one.txt`. It also checks that an unscoped listing errors on the broken ignore file, so scoping is actually applied.\n\n**Selected language**\n- `list_read_only_provider_supports_selected_language_parse_and_structural_search` uses a `Primitives` provider whose file has language `rust` but a location of `generated`, with no extension. Parsing produces an outline where `fn[provided]` resolves. This shows the selected language drives outlining.\n- The same test parses `file.py` with the explicit language `rust` and still resolves `fn[provided]`. This covers the case where the explicit language wins over the extension. It also parses `file.rs` with no explicit language and resolves the outline, which covers the extension fallback.\n- Structural search with query language `rust` finds `call()` at `fn[provided]` with path `generated`. A query with language `python` over the same rust-language file returns a complete, empty result. An unknown query language errors. A malformed rule errors, and a parse that returns a different resource is reported as a provider error.\n- `structural_search_uses_overridden_parses_their_snapshot_and_requested_scope` also checks language-selected structural search over `src/one.rs`.\n\nTogether these tests cover scope preservation for text and structural search, and language selection for outlining and structural search, including both the explicit-language and extension-derived cases. Each has asserted outcomes. I found no requirement-relevant scenario that the tests would let a violating implementation pass.",
  "references": "fb25fa326e584dedcc46b4cb5ffb943c498c41361dc844a88aafec157fd70a3f",
